'''3.''' To come to one opinion or mind; come to an arrangement or understanding; arrive at a settlement.
{{smaller block|''Agree'' with thine adversary quickly. {{right|Mat. v. 25.}}}}
{{smaller block|They ''agree'', he to command, they to obey.{{right|''Selden'', Table-Talk, p. 88.}}}}
{{smaller block|Where an ambiguous question arises between two governments, there is, if they cannot ''agree'', no appeal except to force. {{right|''Macaulay'', Warren Hastings.}}}}
{{smaller block|Didst not thou ''agree'' with me for a penny? {{right|Mat. xx. 13.}}}}
{{smaller block|Make not a city feast of it, to let the meat cool ere we can ''agree'' upon the first place. {{right|''Shak.'', T. of A., iii. 6.}}}}
{{smaller block|Society seems to have ''agreed'' to treat fictions as realities, and realities as fictions. {{right|''Emerson'', Clubs.}}}}
'''4.''' To yield assent; consent; rarely, express concurrence: as, he ''agreed'' to accompany the ambassador.
{{smaller block|''Agree'' to any covenants. {{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. VI., v. 5.}}}}
{{smaller block|''Agree'' with his demands to the point.{{right|''Shak.'', M. for M., iii. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|The tyrant would have ''agreed'' to all that the nation demanded. {{right|''Macaulay'', Burleigh.}}}}
'''B.''' With a thing or things for the subject, in which case ''agree'' now takes no preposition except ''with'' or ''in'' after it, though formerly ''to'' was also so used. '''5.''' To be consistent; harmonize; not to conflict or be repugnant: as, this story ''agrees'' with what has been related by others.
{{smaller block|Their witness ''agreed'' not together. {{right|Mark xiv. 56.}}}}
{{smaller block|When we possess ourselves with the utmost security of the demonstration, that the three angles of a triangle are equal to two right ones, what do we more but perceive that equality to two right ones does necessarily ''agree'' to, and is inseparable from, the three angles of a triangle?{{right|''Locke'', Human Understanding, v. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>A wild-rose roofs the ruined shed,
And that and summer well ''agree''.</poem>}}

readings.c1s2:
{{smaller block|{{right|''Coleridge'', A Day Dream.}}}}
'''6.''' To resemble; be similar; be applicable or appropriate; tally; match; correspond; coincide: as, the picture does not ''agree'' with the original.
{{smaller block|They all ''agree'' in having for their object deliverance from the evils of time.
{{right|''J. F. Clarke'', Ten Great Religions, iii. 5.}}
His system of theology ''agreed'' with that of the Puritans.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., vii.}}}}
'''7.''' To suit; be accommodated or adapted: as, the same food does not ''agree'' with every constitution.—'''8.''' In ''gram.'', to correspond in number, case, gender, or person: as, a verb must ''agree'' with its subject. ='''Syn.''' {{smaller|To accord (with), concur (in), subscribe (to), promise, engage, undertake. See list under ''accede''.}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To settle; determine; arrange.
{{smaller block|<poem>He saw from far . . .
Some troublous uprore or contentious fray,
Whereto he drew in hast it to ''agree''.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., II. iv. 3.}}
I do believe the two Pretenders had, privately, ''agreed'' the matter beforehand.
{{right|''Gray''.}}
[This use of the verb ''agree'' is now obsolete except in the impersonal phrase ''it is agreed'', and in a few legal and business expressions: as, the account has been ''agreed''.
<poem>''It is'' thus ''agreed''
That peaceful truce shall be proclaim’d in France.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. VI., v. 4.]}}}}
'''2†.''' To agree with; suit.
{{smaller block|If harm ''agree'' me, wherto pleyne I thenne?
{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, i. 409.}}}}
'''Case agreed''' or '''stated.''' {{smaller|See ''case''<sup>1</sup>.}}

