'''agreeable''' (a-grē′a-bl), ''a.'' [< ME. ''agreable'', < OF. ''agreable'' (F. ''agréable''), < ''agreer'': see ''agree'', ''v.''] '''1.''' Suitable; conformable; correspondent: as, conduct ''agreeable'' to the moral law.
{{smaller block|Though they embraced not this practice of burning, yet entertained they many ceremonies ''agreeable'' unto Greek and Roman obsequies.
{{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Urn-Burial, i.}}}}
{{smaller block|[In this sense ''agreeable'' is sometimes incorrectly used for ''agreeably'': as, ''agreeable'' to the order of the day, the house took up the report of the committee.]}}
'''2.''' Pleasing, either to the mind or to the senses; to one’s liking: as, ''agreeable'' manners; fruit ''agreeable'' to the taste.
{{smaller block|There was something extremely ''agreeable'' in the cheerful flow of animal spirits of the little man.
{{right|''Irving'', Sketch-Book, p. 260.}}}}
{{smaller block|My idea of an ''agreeable'' person, said Hugo Bohun, is a person who agrees with me.
{{right|''Disraeli''.}}}}
'''3.''' Willing or ready to agree or consent: now used only or chiefly as a colloquialism.
{{smaller block|These Frenchmen give unto the said captain of Calais a great sum of money, so that he will be but content and ''agreeable'' that they may enter into the said town.
{{right|''Latimer''.}}}}

