{{smaller block|I’ll meet you there, and bring my wife that is to be. . . . You’re ''agreeable?''
{{right|''Dickens.''}}}}
'''4†.''' Agreeing one with another; concordant.
{{smaller block|These manifold and ''agreeable'' testimonies of the olde and new writers.
{{right|''Author of'' 1596, ''quoted by'' F. ''Hall.''}}}}
='''Syn. 1.''' Fitting, befitting, appropriate, consonant (with).—'''2.''' ''Pleasing'', etc. See ''pleasant''.

'''agreeableness''' (ạ-grē′ạ-bl-nes), ''n.'' The state or quality of being agreeable. {{smaller|(''a'') Suitableness; conformity; consistency: as, the ''agreeableness'' of virtue to the laws of God. (''b'') The quality of pleasing; that quality which gives satisfaction or moderate pleasure to the mind or senses: as, ''agreeableness'' of manners; there is an ''agreeableness'' in the taste of certain fruits.}}
{{smaller block|We have entered into a contract of mutual ''agreeableness'' for the space of an evening.
{{right|''Jane Austen'', Northanger Abbey, x.}}}}
{{smaller|(''c''†) Concordance; harmony; agreement.}}
{{smaller block|The ''agreeableness'' between man and other parts of creation.
{{right|''Grew'', Cosmologia Sacra.}}}}

'''agreeably''' (ạ-grē′ạ-bli), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''agreablely'': see ''agreeable'' and ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] In an agreeable manner. {{smaller|(''a'') Suitably; consistently; conformably. See remark under ''agreeable'', 1.}}
{{smaller block|The effect of which is, that marriages grow less frequent, ''agreeably'' to the maxim above laid down.
{{right|''Paley.''}}
Reason requires us, when we speak of Christianity, to expound the phrase ''agreeably'' to history, if we mean to claim on its behalf the authority of civilized man.
{{right|''Gladstone'', Might of Right, p. 189.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') Pleasingly; in an agreeable manner; in a manner to give pleasure: as, to be ''agreeably'' entertained with a discourse.}}

